#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "997d255f-89af-5563-b10f-eecaae62d8c0" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule for Exponents With Integer Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "3625f2a9-3103-551e-b350-b0c83d9b241f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13039" :question/problem "Express $5(5^2) \\times 5^4$ as a base raised to a single exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n5(5^2) = 5^1 \\times 5^2\n$$\n\nSo our expression can be written as\n$5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4$\n\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents:\n\n$$\n5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4 = 5^{1+2+4} = 5^7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aa91f9b-e324-557c-9a6b-fb3389bbeaf8" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule for Exponents With Fractional or Decimal Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "edac9b20-71bf-544e-8e28-833c376097d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13040" :question/problem "Express $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2$ as a base raised to a single exponent." :question/worked-solution "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2+2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908d5434-7533-5d33-bc0d-99f4cc9dd44b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Products with Negative Exponents Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "81027692-8570-59a6-a6f9-b31211baa681" :question/math-academy-id "e-13041" :question/problem "Evaluate $2^2\\times 2^{-5}$." :question/worked-solution "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n2^2\\times 2^{-5} =2^{2+(-5)} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-3}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-3} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$"}]}
